WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.204
npm audit and similar tools are useful
because they compare the dependency tree

2
00:00:08.284 --> 00:00:12.423
against known vulnerability advisories.

3
00:00:12.503 --> 00:00:20.207
They can show affected packages, severity
labels, vulnerable version ranges,

4
00:00:20.287 --> 00:00:23.047
and possible upgrade paths.

5
00:00:23.127 --> 00:00:26.116
That visibility is important,

6
00:00:26.196 --> 00:00:32.060
especially in projects where dependencies
change quickly.

7
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:37.545
But an audit report is an input to
engineering judgment,

8
00:00:37.625 --> 00:00:41.419
not a complete risk decision by itself.

9
00:00:41.499 --> 00:00:49.893
Triage should consider whether the
vulnerable package is present in production code,

10
00:00:49.973 --> 00:00:54.572
whether the vulnerable function is
reachable,

11
00:00:54.652 --> 00:00:59.596
whether the package runs only during
development,

12
00:00:59.676 --> 00:01:04.046
what privilege or data exposure is
involved,

13
00:01:04.126 --> 00:01:10.335
and whether a fix is available without
breaking compatibility.

14
00:01:10.415 --> 00:01:17.659
A critical finding in an unreachable
development-only tool may be handled

15
00:01:17.739 --> 00:01:23.718
differently from a moderate finding in a
public request path.

16
00:01:23.798 --> 00:01:30.237
Severity labels help prioritize, but they
do not replace context.

17
00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:38.481
Blindly running npm audit fix can be risky
because automated remediation may change

18
00:01:38.561 --> 00:01:46.840
package versions, alter transitive
dependencies, or introduce breaking behavior.

19
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:54.623
A safer workflow is to review the proposed
change, understand the upgrade path,

20
00:01:54.703 --> 00:02:01.602
run tests, compare lockfile movement, and
document any accepted risk.

21
00:02:01.682 --> 00:02:08.121
When no patch is available, teams may need
compensating controls,

22
00:02:08.201 --> 00:02:12.341
package replacement, feature isolation,

23
00:02:12.421 --> 00:02:17.940
or a tracked exception with an owner and
expiration date.
